Walk me through your experience with Linux commands.
In my daily work, I use Linux commands to manage servers, check system health, monitor resources and troubleshoot basic issues.

Some commands I regularly work with are:
1. File Management: ls, cd, pwd, mkdir, cp, mv, rm – to manage files and directories.
2. User Management: useradd, passwd, usermod, id – to create and manage users.
3. Permissions: chmod, chown – to manage file and directory permissions.
4. System Monitoring: top, free -h, df -h, du -sh, uptime – to check CPU, memory, disk usage and system load.
5. Service Management: systemctl, service – to start, stop, restart and check services like SSH, Apache and Nginx.
6. Log Management: tail -f, cat, less, grep – to check logs and troubleshoot application issues.
7. Network Troubleshooting: ping, curl, ss -tulnp, ip addr – to check connectivity, IP addresses and listening ports.
Real-time production example:

For example, if an application is not accessible, I first check whether the server is running and then check CPU, memory and disk usage.

After that, I check the application service using systemctl status, verify the required port using ss -tulnp, and check application logs using tail -f.

Based on the error, I try to identify the issue and take the required action. If it requires application-level changes, I coordinate with the application team.

Overall, I use Linux commands regularly for server management, monitoring and basic troubleshooting.
